Lightningpanda patch 0025: no lock round trip and no atom-table probe for calls from callbacks.

- JSLockIfNeeded (API/JSLockIfNeeded.h): JSObjectMakeWrapper, JSObjectGet/SetProperty and
  JSObjectGet/SetPrivateProperty take a JSLockHolder only when the thread does not hold the
  VM's lock already. From inside a callback (where patch 0009 keeps the lock) JSLockHolder only
  bumped the lock count but paid a VM ref, an apiLock ref, their releases and a failed tryLock.
- JSStringCreateAtom: a copy of an API string that keeps the identifier OpaqueJSString::identifier()
  makes for it (the atom, with a reference never released) and reuses it while it is still an
  atom of the calling VM's table, instead of hashing the characters and probing the atom table
  on every property-name API call (a hidden-property write per event dispatch and listener
  call, the current-event slot, ...). API context groups have their own atom tables, switched
  in on lock entry, so the atom is remembered with its table; a string used with another VM
  takes the usual path, and once the table is destroyed (its destructor clears the atom flag)
  the next VM's atom is remembered. Releasing the atom could crash under another current
  table, hence the leaked reference: one string per name per table. Use on one thread.
